ly-now-downsizing-the-3970-sq-ft-forever-house-is-asking-2-45-million/articleshow/134638215.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/garth-stein-bought-a-1924-seattle-house-in-2001-and-spent-25-years-restoring-and-updating-it-while-raising-three-children-with-the-family-now-downsizing-the-3970-sq-ft-forever-house-is-asking-2-45-million/articleshow/134638215.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-04T01:50:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Real Estate Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134638357,imgsize-332267.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Tony Stewart’s 20,000-sq-ft Indiana ranch drops from $30M to $18.9M after two price cuts]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134635789.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Tony Stewart’s Hidden Hollow Ranch in Indiana has returned to the market for $18.9 million. The 415-acre property has a 19,713-square-foot main house with six bedrooms, an 8,700-gallon aquarium, indoor stream, bowling alley and golf simulator.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/in-2011-tony-stewart-built-a-nearly-20000-sq-ft-home-on-415-indiana-acres-with-a-waterfall-8700-gallon-aquarium-and-bowling-alley-after-asking-30-million-it-is-now-listed-at-18-9-million/articleshow/134635789.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/in-2011-tony-stewart-built-a-nearly-20000-sq-ft-home-on-415-indiana-acres-with-a-waterfall-8700-gallon-aquarium-and-bowling-alley-after-asking-30-million-it-is-now-listed-at-18-9-million/articleshow/134635789.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-04T00:50:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Real Estate Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134636088,imgsize-78494.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[O'Connor ranching legacy began in 1834; 6,409 acres of Texas prairie protected 191 years later]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134637437.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[The historic T.M. O'Connor Ranch in Texas has gained a notable conservation easement, protecting nearly 6,409 acres of precious coastal prairie while enabling ranching operations to persist. Made feasible through a combination of federal and private funding under the RESTORE program, this area plays a pivotal role in supporting over 300 distinct wildlife species and rare flora. This agreement showcases a united front for conservation efforts alongside sustainable farming solutions.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1834-the-oconnor-familys-texas-ranching-legacy-began-191-years-later-an-8-863-million-deal-protected-6409-acres-of-coastal-prairie-while-keeping-it-a-working-cattle-ranch/articleshow/134637437.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1834-the-oconnor-familys-texas-ranching-legacy-began-191-years-later-an-8-863-million-deal-protected-6409-acres-of-coastal-prairie-while-keeping-it-a-working-cattle-ranch/articleshow/134637437.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-03T23:55:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134637596,imgsize-76227.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Boy, 6, wrote ‘Chocolate Bar’ for friend with rare liver disorder; book raised $1 million]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134636457.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[At 6, Los Angeles boy Dylan Siegel wrote and sold a book calledChocolate Barto help fund research into his best friend Jonah’s rare liver disorder. What began as a child’s idea grew into a fundraising effort that had raised $1 million by 2014.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/at-6-los-angeles-boy-dylan-siegel-wrote-chocolate-bar-to-help-his-best-friend-jonah-who-had-a-rare-liver-disorder-two-years-later-the-book-had-raised-1-million-for-research-into-the-disease/articleshow/134636457.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/at-6-los-angeles-boy-dylan-siegel-wrote-chocolate-bar-to-help-his-best-friend-jonah-who-had-a-rare-liver-disorder-two-years-later-the-book-had-raised-1-million-for-research-into-the-disease/articleshow/134636457.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-03T23:50:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134636668,imgsize-121456.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Iowa family’s 84-year farming legacy ends as 1,209 acres go up for auction]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134641582.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[On October 30, a landmark auction will take place for the 1,209-acre farmland portfolio owned by the Woods family in Fayette County. This well-preserved parcel boasts high-quality soil, attracting a variety of potential buyers—from local farmers to investors and recreational users. The hybrid auction setup will enable competitive bidding on individual lots, marking the culmination of the family’s 84-year legacy with the land.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/an-iowa-family-lived-on-and-worked-its-farmland-for-84-years-as-tom-woods-gradually-expanded-the-operation-now-1209-acres-are-being-split-into-13-tracts-and-auctioned-ending-a-family-land-chapter/articleshow/134641582.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/an-iowa-family-lived-on-and-worked-its-farmland-for-84-years-as-tom-woods-gradually-expanded-the-operation-now-1209-acres-are-being-split-into-13-tracts-and-auctioned-ending-a-family-land-chapter/articleshow/134641582.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-03T22:59:27+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134641712,imgsize-198587.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Sally Quinn and Ben Bradlee bought Grey Gardens for $220,000; it later sold for $15.5M]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134641195.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In a momentous deal for historic preservation, Sally Quinn and Ben Bradlee acquired the storied Grey Gardens estate in 1979 for $220,000. Following a meticulous restoration, it emerged as a cultural hallmark tied to the Beales. Its remarkable sale for $15.5 million in December 2017 underscores the lasting interest in uniquely significant properties, concluding nearly forty years of history for this iconic location.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/in-1979-sally-quinn-and-ben-bradlee-paid-220000-for-new-yorks-decaying-grey-gardens-and-restored-it-38-years-later-the-6000-sq-ft-east-hampton-estate-sold-for-15-5-million/articleshow/134641195.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/in-1979-sally-quinn-and-ben-bradlee-paid-220000-for-new-yorks-decaying-grey-gardens-and-restored-it-38-years-later-the-6000-sq-ft-east-hampton-estate-sold-for-15-5-million/articleshow/134641195.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-03T22:58:44+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Real Estate Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134641228,imgsize-134188.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[‘Will sign those checks’: Trump repeats $5,000 promise to US adults if Republicans win 2026 midterms]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134663445.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/will-sign-those-checks-trump-repeats-5000-promise-to-us-adults-if-republicans-win-2026-midterms/articleshow/134663445.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/will-sign-those-checks-trump-repeats-5000-promise-to-us-adults-if-republicans-win-2026-midterms/articleshow/134663445.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-03T22:38:17+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Aashish Vashistha</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134663558,imgsize-52102.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Four North Carolina homes repaired after Helene face demolition after FEMA buyouts]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134636925.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In the aftermath of Hurricane Helene, four homes in the Cottages at Cypress Run neighborhood in Hendersonville face demolition due to severe flooding. Previously, these homes were repaired through homeowner efforts and community volunteerism. To address the persistent flood threats, homeowners have chosen FEMA's voluntary buyouts, aligning with the Hazard Mitigation Grant Program's initiative to convert affected land into green spaces, thereby reducing future flood risks.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/four-north-carolina-homes-damaged-by-helene-were-repaired-with-owners-money-and-volunteer-help-two-years-later-fema-buyouts-mean-the-livable-houses-will-be-demolished-and-returned-to-green-space/articleshow/134636925.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/four-north-carolina-homes-damaged-by-helene-were-repaired-with-owners-money-and-volunteer-help-two-years-later-fema-buyouts-mean-the-livable-houses-will-be-demolished-and-returned-to-green-space/articleshow/134636925.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-03T22:02:55+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134637007,imgsize-166110.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Queensland dairy farmer opened four campsites after drought forced her family to sell 40 cows]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134646515.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Agritourism is gaining momentum among Australian farmers struggling with drought-related financial pressures. Landowners like Libby Rough are transforming their properties into camping havens, creating alternative income streams. This shift not only aids in sustaining their farms during tough times but also promotes local agricultural education among visitors. Moreover, local authorities are streamlining regulations to encourage this thriving sector, enhancing community engagement with farming practices.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/queensland-dairy-farmer-libby-rough-opened-just-four-campsites-after-drought-forced-her-family-to-sell-40-cows-by-january-2022-the-riverside-pitches-had-brought-in-more-than-a50000/articleshow/134646515.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/queensland-dairy-farmer-libby-rough-opened-just-four-campsites-after-drought-forced-her-family-to-sell-40-cows-by-january-2022-the-riverside-pitches-had-brought-in-more-than-a50000/articleshow/134646515.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-03T22: